.TH FOPEN 3crunch "" "Crunch" "Crunch Library Manual"
.SH NAME
fopen \- open a Crunch file stream
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <stdio.h>
.PP
.BI "struct FILE* fopen(const char* " path ", const char* " mode ");"
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.BR fopen ()
opens a path and allocates a Crunch stream. The initial mode character is
.B r
(read an existing file),
.B w
(write, creating or truncating), or
.B a
(write, creating if necessary, with writes forced to the end).
An optional
.B +
enables both reading and writing.
.B b
is accepted and has no effect on Linux.
.B e
sets close-on-exec.
With
.B w
only,
.B x
requires creation of a new file.
Each modifier may appear once, in any order.
New files use permissions 0666 modified by the process umask.
.SH RETURN VALUE
Returns a stream pointer on success, or null with
.B errno
set on failure. Invalid modes or null arguments fail with
.BR EINVAL .
.SH NOTES
The stream belongs to Crunch and must be released with
.BR fclose (3crunch).
Its fields are private and it is not compatible with a C library
.BR FILE .
Stream I/O is unbuffered.
Crunch's allocator and errno are not thread-safe.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR fclose (3crunch),
.BR fseek (3crunch),
.BR fread (3crunch),
.BR fwrite (3crunch),
.BR open (3crunch)
